The logic:
$S(j, 0)$ is the start time of potion $j$ at wizard 0.
$T(j, i) = S(j, 0) + \sum_{k=0}^i skill[k] \cdot mana[j]$
Constraints:
1. For $i > 0$, $S(j, i) = T(j, i-1)$ (Immediate pass)
2. For $i \ge 0$, $S(j, i) \ge T(j-1, i)$ (Wizard availability)

For $i=0$: $S(j, 0) \ge T(j-1, 0) = S(j-1, 0) + P(0, j-1)$
For $i>0$: $T(j, i-1) \ge T(j-1, i) \implies S(j, 0) + P(i-1, j) \ge S(j-1, 0) + P(i, j-1)$
$\implies S(j, 0) \ge S(j-1, 0) + P(i, j-1) - P(i-1, j)$

So $S(j, 0) = S(j-1, 0) + \max(P(0, j-1), \max_{i=1}^{n-1} (P(i, j-1) - P(i-1, j)))$.
This is exactly what I implemented.

I'll do a final check on the constraints: $n, m \le 5000$. The $O(nm)$ approach might be slow in Python if not careful.
$5000^2 = 25,000,000$. In Python, this loop might take a few seconds.

